---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const phCfg = {
  curves: [{ expr: '-log(x)', label: 'pH of a strong acid solution', color: '#d9480f', width: 2.2 }],
  x: { min: 1e-14, max: 1, label: 'H⁺ concentration (mol/L) — log scale' },
  y: { min: 0, max: 14, label: 'pH' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 350,
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/chemistry/foundational/acids-bases-and-ph">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Acids donate H⁺ ions; bases accept them (Brønsted's 1923 definition — the useful one). Water
    itself does both, very slightly: in pure water, H⁺ and OH⁻ each sit at 10⁻⁷ mol/L, so the
    neutral point is pH 7. Because concentrations span 14 orders of magnitude, chemistry reports
    them as <strong>pH = −log₁₀[H⁺]</strong> — one digit per tenfold change in strength.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the pH scale is a log ruler — each unit down is a tenfold acidifier" widget="plotter" config={phCfg} />

  <p>
    Read the curve from right to left: lemon juice (pH 2) has 100,000× the H⁺ of neutral water;
    oven cleaner (pH 13–14) runs the other way. Your blood holds pH 7.35–7.45 — drifting 0.4
    beyond either edge is life-threatening, which is why blood carries CO₂/bicarbonate buffers
    (a later lesson's equilibrium in action).
  </p>

  <h2>The working facts</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Strong vs concentrated are different axes.</strong> HCl is a strong acid (every molecule donates) but can be dilute; acetic acid is weak (only ~1% donates) even at high concentration.</li>
    <li><strong>Neutralisation:</strong> H⁺ + OH⁻ → H₂O, releasing ~57 kJ/mol. Titration counts moles of acid by dripping base until an indicator flips — stoichiometry with a colour signal.</li>
    <li><strong>Buffers resist change:</strong> a weak acid plus its salt absorbs added H⁺ or OH⁻. Your blood, seawater and shampoo all rely on buffer systems.</li>
    <li><strong>pH in the wild:</strong> rain is naturally ~5.6 (dissolved CO₂); acid rain dips to 4; the ocean has shifted from 8.2 toward 8.1 since industrialisation — 30% more H⁺, quietly dissolving shellfish shells.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Brønsted & Lowry (1923); OpenStax, Chemistry 2e (Acid–Base Equilibria); NOAA ocean acidification data.</p>
</TopicPage>
